    "q-134595": {
      "dom_id": "question-134595",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Polygon $A$ is congruent to polygon\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         (enter the polygon's label).",
      "worked_solution": "In general, congruent polygons have the same shape. In other words:\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's compare polygons $B$ and $D$ to $A.$\n\n- The polygon $A$ is an octagon (it has $8$ sides). So, the polygons $B$ and $D$ can't be congruent to the polygon $A$ because they do not have $8$ sides.\n\n- On the other hand, $A$ and $C$ have the same shape: they have congruent corresponding sides, and since they are regular polygons, congruent corresponding angles. This implies that $A≅C.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "dom_id": "",
          "tag": "mathquill",
          "choices": [],
          "dom_index": 0,
          "disabled": false,
          "input_html": "<div class=\"matheditor-wrapper-answer\" style=\"min-width: 20px; margin-left: 5px; margin-right: 5px;\">\n                                             \n                                        <div class=\"mq-editable-field mq-math-mode\" style=\"border: none; outline: none;\"><span class=\"mq-textarea\"><textarea autocapitalize=\"off\" autocomplete=\"off\" autocorrect=\"off\" spellcheck=\"false\" x-palm-disable-ste-all=\"true\" style=\"\" disabled=\"\"></textarea></span><span class=\"mq-root-block\" mathquill-block-id=\"3\"><var mathquill-command-id=\"23\">C</var></span></div></div>",
